@N@Ninap430. The World Cup is the most well loved sport in the world, Trinidad which has one of the highest crime rates of all countries goes down to near 0% during the World Cup as does most of the Caribbean.

Nobody is forgetting this and America will face extreme consequences for this in the form of massive industry drops especially that of tourism and international sales.

I would be surprised if the US ever actually fully recovers from this in terms of image as ruining a sport that unites whole countries of people to watch and gets BILLIONS of views each time its on tends to be unrepairable.

@m@mikewolf-x6t1.) Your grammar is atrocious. I’m pretty sure you meant to say “you have no idea what you are talking about” instead you said “you have idea what you are talking about” which yes, yes I do have an idea of what I  talking about. I know what I am talking about. You, however, are another story. 

2.) This ref is FAR from the first time I have seen a non government employee granted a diplomatic passport in countries around the world. He’s not even the first ref in FIFA World Cup history to be granted one. Is it relatively rare? Yes, but this is not some brand new, never been done before, ground breaking thing. 

3.) He DID have a valid visa. When he boarded the plane to come here he had one. It was revoked after he landed in Miami. DHS is trying to claim “vetting concerns” but that should have come long before he was ever granted one while actually being vetted, and the claims DHS has made have had ZERO evidence provided to give such claims any level of credibility. Essentially, it’s BS discrimination.
